# Netlify redirect/rewrite rules — evaluated top to bottom, first match wins.
# Rules without "!" are skipped when a real file exists at the path, so built
# assets, robots.txt and favicons are always served as files.
# NOTE: netlify.toml [[context.production.redirects]] is silently IGNORED by
# Netlify — redirects cannot be scoped to deploy contexts. Keep SPA rules here.

# Dynamic sitemap — served by Netlify Function (also in netlify.toml for netlify dev).
/sitemap.xml  /.netlify/functions/sitemap  200!

# Netlify's default domain served a full, indexable copy of the site (HTTP 200),
# competing with afrinia.org as a duplicate. Send it to the real domain.
# Matches only this exact host — deploy previews (<id>--afinia.netlify.app)
# are unaffected. www and http are already redirected by Netlify domain settings.
https://afinia.netlify.app/*  https://afrinia.org/:splat  301!

# Legacy /blog index → French listing (French is the site default / x-default).
# /blog/:slug stays client-side (see below): the slug's language is unknown here.
/blog  /fr/blog  301

# ── Known app routes → index.html with HTTP 200 ─────────────────────────────
# WHY a whitelist: the SPA serves the same index.html for every URL, so unknown
# URLs used to answer 200 and draw "404" in JavaScript — Google reports those as
# "Soft 404". Listing the real routes lets everything else return a true 404.
# MUST mirror the <Route> list in src/App.tsx: a route missing here is served
# with status 404 and will drop out of Google. Add new routes here and to
# scripts/check-live-site.mjs in the same commit.
/                 /index.html  200
/about            /index.html  200
/contact          /index.html  200
/audio            /index.html  200
/builders         /index.html  200
/builders/*       /index.html  200
/en/blog          /index.html  200
/fr/blog          /index.html  200
/en/blog/*        /index.html  200
/fr/blog/*        /index.html  200
# TEMP: /blog/:slug is redirected client-side to the browser language (App.tsx
# BlogRedirect). Real fix: server-side 301 using the slug's language, in the
# Next.js redirect map (CLAUDE.md §10, M5).
/blog/*           /index.html  200
/privacy          /index.html  200
/terms            /index.html  200
/unsubscribed     /index.html  200
/profile          /index.html  200
/settings         /index.html  200
/admin            /index.html  200
/admin/*          /index.html  200

# ── Everything else → HTTP 404 ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# No catch-all rule here, on purpose: Netlify answers unmatched URLs with
# dist/404.html (a copy of the app, emitted by vite.config.ts) and status 404.
# A `/* /index.html 404` rule would outrank function paths and break /sitemap.xml.
# Unmatched includes retired pages of the previous site (/services, /products,
# /pricing, /solutions, /example-systems, /built-by, /industrial-analytics,
# /outreachos): they have no equivalent content, so 404 is the honest answer
# for crawlers (redirecting them to the homepage is itself a soft 404).
# Visitors still see the app, which sends those legacy paths to the homepage.
# Known limit until Next.js (M2): an unknown article slug under /fr/blog/* or
# /en/blog/* still answers 200, because only the database knows which exist.
